    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Why Does Funchal Have No Big Sandy Beach?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>9 December 2026</span><span>6 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Because Madeira is a young volcanic island with a steep coast and a narrow underwater shelf, so there is little sand to begin with and nowhere for it to settle. The sea here makes pebbles, which is why Funchal&#x27;s beaches are stony and the golden beaches at Calheta and Machico were built with imported sand.</p>

      <h2>What is the short geological answer?</h2><p>Three things work together, and none of them is going to change in our lifetime.</p><ol><li><strong>The rock is young basalt.</strong> The part of Madeira above the sea rose and was built in roughly the last six to seven million years, and its youngest lavas are only about 7,000 years old. Basalt is hard. When cliffs crack and fall, the sea rolls the blocks for years and rounds them into stones long before it grinds them into sand.</li><li><strong>The streams carry little most of the year.</strong> A 2024 University of Lisbon thesis on the shelf off the south coast found that most of the sediment there comes from crumbling cliffs, because the streams run with so little water. When they do run hard, in winter storms, they bring down stones and boulders, not fine sand. After the flood of 20 February 2010, about 200,000 cubic metres of rubble were dumped on Funchal's seafront.</li><li><strong>The shelf is narrow and the slopes are steep.</strong> Researchers describe the sea floor around the archipelago as narrow shelves with steep slopes. On young volcanic islands like this, studies in the Azores find that storms push loose sediment off the shelf and down the slopes, so fine grains rarely stay near the shore for long.</li></ol><p>The result is a coast of what Madeirans call calhau: a shore of rounded stones. The old Elucidário Madeirense describes these beaches as loose, black, mostly round stones that roar when the backwash drags them. Stand on Praia Formosa as a wave pulls back and you will hear exactly that.</p><h2>Why does Porto Santo have a long sandy beach, then?</h2><p>Because it is a different island with a different history. Porto Santo is much older than Madeira, about 11 to 14 million years, and it has a broader shelf. Its beach runs for about 9 kilometres, and studies of its old dunes, some up to 50 metres thick, show they were built from sand blown off that shelf when sea level was lower during the ice ages. Madeira never had a shelf like that to borrow from. If sand matters to you, our guide to <a href="/posts/can-you-go-to-porto-santo-by-boat-from-madeira">getting to Porto Santo by boat</a> covers the ferry.</p><h2>Where did the golden sand at Calheta and Machico come from?</h2><p>From North Africa, by ship. Both beaches were built behind breakwaters, which hold the sand in place, and both are worth knowing about if a child in your group has been promised sandcastles.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Beach</th><th>Opened</th><th>Size, roughly</th><th>Where the sand came from</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Calheta, south-west coast</td><td>2004, the first artificial beach on Madeira</td><td>About 100 m, between two breakwaters</td><td>Yellow sand that Visit Madeira gives as from Morocco. Other sources say Western Sahara.</td></tr><tr><td>Machico, east coast</td><td>29 September 2008</td><td>About 125 m long and 70 m wide</td><td>Shipped from Laayoune, in Western Sahara, a territory mostly controlled by Morocco</td></tr><tr><td>Banda d'Além, Machico</td><td>Not confirmed</td><td>About 70 m</td><td>Yellow sand, given as from Morocco</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Imported sand is not a one-off job. Calheta has had further shipments of sand since it opened.</p><h2>Is there any natural sand on Madeira at all?</h2><p>A little, and almost all of it is dark. Prainha, near Caniçal at the eastern end, is a small beach of natural volcanic sand with a coppery colour. On the north coast, Seixal has a black sand beach that formed naturally beside its harbour. In Funchal itself, Praia do Areeiro, one of the beaches that make up Praia Formosa, is black sand mixed with pebbles. For why the island's sand is black rather than gold, see <a href="/posts/why-does-madeira-have-black-sand">why Madeira has black sand</a>.</p><h2>Where do people in Funchal swim instead?</h2><p>From pebbles, from lidos and from rock pools along the seafront. These are the usual choices:</p><ul><li><strong>Praia Formosa</strong>, west of the hotel zone: the largest public beach in Funchal, four beaches joined into about 2 kilometres of rounded pebbles and black sand, with a seafront promenade.</li><li><strong>Complexo Balnear do Lido</strong>: two saltwater pools and access to the sea. It opened in 1932, closed after the 2010 flood and reopened in the mid-2010s. Its season runs from June to September.</li><li><strong>Ponta Gorda</strong>, a little further west: two more saltwater pools on the rocks.</li><li><strong>Doca do Cavacas and Poças do Gomes</strong>: natural volcanic rock pools with a way into the open sea.</li><li><strong>Barreirinha, São Tiago and Almirante Reis</strong>, at the old town end: a lido and small pebble beaches.</li></ul><h2>Is a pebble beach worse for swimming?</h2><p>For some people, yes. If you want to lie on soft sand with a toddler digging beside you, Funchal will disappoint, and Calheta, Machico or Porto Santo are the honest answer. Pebble beaches also shelve steeply, so the water gets deep within a few steps, and getting in and out over rolling stones in a swell is clumsy without water shoes.</p><p>The trade is the water itself. With no sand to stir up, the sea off a stony shore stays clear even close in, and it is deep enough to swim properly almost at once.</p><p>You can see the whole pattern from the sea. On the run back into Funchal from the west, Chifbay's boat passes Praia Formosa, Ponta Gorda and the Lido one after another: a grey stony shore, then pools cut into the rocks, then hotels on the cliffs. Not one stretch of yellow in sight.</p><h2>Can you swim off a beach without walking over the stones?</h2><p>Yes, from a boat. The Chifbay <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip west of Funchal</a> stops for swims off two pebble shores, the fajã under the cliffs at Fajã dos Padres and the town beach at Ribeira Brava, and you get in from the boat in deep, clear sea without touching the bottom. Fajã dos Padres is also where the paddle boards come out. If that stop is what interests you, the <a href="/faja-dos-padres-boat-trip">Fajã dos Padres boat trip</a> page explains it, and our guide to <a href="/posts/beaches-you-pass-on-a-boat-trip-west-of-funchal">the beaches you pass sailing west of Funchal</a> names the rest of the shore.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Is there a sandy beach in Funchal?</h3><p>Not a big golden one. Funchal's beaches are pebble beaches, and the largest, Praia Formosa, mixes rounded stones with patches of black sand.</p><h3>Which Madeira beaches have imported sand?</h3><p>Calheta, opened in 2004, and Machico, opened in September 2008, both with yellow sand shipped from North Africa. Banda d'Além in Machico also has yellow sand.</p><h3>Where is the nearest golden sand beach to Funchal?</h3><p>On Madeira, Machico on the east coast and Calheta on the south-west coast. The only long natural golden beach is on Porto Santo, about 9 km of sand.</p><h3>Do I need water shoes in Funchal?</h3><p>They help on the pebble beaches, where stones roll underfoot and the shore drops steeply. At the lidos you swim in pools or enter the sea from paved areas.</p>

      <blockquote>Madeira&#x27;s beaches are made of stones still on their way to becoming sand.</blockquote>
    </article>
    <p class="pref-src" data-pref-source><span>Enjoying our Madeira guides?</span> <a href="https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=chifbay.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="#4285F4" d="M23.5 12.3c0-.8-.1-1.6-.2-2.3H12v4.5h6.5a5.6 5.6 0 0 1-2.4 3.6v3h3.9c2.2-2.1 3.5-5.1 3.5-8.8z"/><path fill="#34A853" d="M12 24c3.2 0 6-1.1 7.9-2.9l-3.9-3c-1.1.7-2.4 1.2-4 1.2-3.1 0-5.7-2.1-6.6-4.9h-4v3.1A12 12 0 0 0 12 24z"/><path fill="#FBBC05" d="M5.4 14.4a7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-4.7V6.6h-4a12 12 0 0 0 0 10.8l4-3z"/><path fill="#EA4335" d="M12 4.8c1.7 0 3.3.6 4.6 1.8l3.4-3.4A12 12 0 0 0 1.4 6.6l4 3.1C6.3 6.9 8.9 4.8 12 4.8z"/></svg>Add Chifbay as a preferred source on Google</a></p>

